A||||5||Joe Torma||Air Conditioning Experts...help?!||||5||
Z||000000||Joe Torma||07-22-2001||08:30 PM||||Two questions:<P>I am adapting my 3400series A/C compressor to the existing fiero system.  To do this I have to cut the new and old rubber lines and splice them together.  hopfully someone will do this for me.<P>Ok now to the issue.  On the V6 system there is 2 pressure switches...a cycling(no not bicycle) switch and a high pressure cut out in the compressor.  I no longer have the high pressure switch.  What do I do?  I guess the system could blow up without it?!  Does anyone know of a high pressure switch...does the 4cyl have one?<P><BR>Thanks in advance,<BR>Joe||||reg||5
Z||000001||cadaver||07-23-2001||12:35 AM||||You could get some fittings and put it inline.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-23-2001||08:44 PM||||You need to backup a bit.....<P>You're asking the wrong question. You need to find out which compressor it is. Many of the newer compressors don't use pressure cycle switches for the clutch because they don't cycle the clutch.<P>DA-V5 is one of the common GM ones in this group. It is variable displacement and spins full time. It uses less HP than the old fixed displacement A6 family and you don't have surging as the old clutch cycles.<P>To make the new hoses you have to find an AC or Hydraulic hose shop that can put the aluminum parts into new barrier type AC hose. This should not be very expensive. Make bloody sure of the lenght the new hose has to be. A little long is better than any short. Short hoses will break things before they pull apart.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe Torma||07-23-2001||09:05 PM||||See, that's why I need an expert!  Dunno what type it is...it's the one off my '97 3400.  Ok, is it safe to run it without the high pressure cutout then?  It cuts out the pressure @ like 425psi.  I think the PCM controls most of the cycling in the 3400.<P>Ugh, what a pain.<P>Joe<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||07-24-2001||07:53 PM||||You need to ID the compressor. Look the original vehicle up in a parts book or take pictures of it and post them.<P>I don't recomend you do the wire changes or refrigerant loading yourself. Find someone that knows what they are doing. <P>The new system is going to need to be charged by pressure. If the compressor is variable displacement then there are special rules for charging. If you don't follow them then the thing will probably be over charged. Not good.<P>There are several wire issues. Do them wrong and you'll have several problems....<BR>1. the engine won't run right. The ECM needs to know when the clutch is running. If this is now a carbed motor you'll have to install something to pull the idle up when the compressor is on or it will likely stall allot.<BR>2. you could fry the ECM<BR>3. the high pressure limit won't work and the system could burst. Especially if you over charge it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||07-24-2001||08:12 PM||||Ok, I'll get the # off the compressor. and get pictures.<P>I'll probably find an local A/C shop to charge it.<P>About the Overcharging...I'll see if the service manual says anything.<P>I have a Tec-II...like stock, it compensates with the IAC for idle quality.  <P>---3. the high pressure limit won't work and the system could burst. Especially if you over charge it.<P>That's what I'm afraid of!!!<P>What do all you engine swappers do?  Keep the stock compressor?  Delete it?  I can't...I would have 3 idler pulleys!!<BR>||||reg||1
